{"headline": "Unidentified projectile strikes King Khalid International Airport in Riyadh, Saudi Arabia", "pages": 
[
  {
    "text": "An unidentified projectile hit terminal 4 at King Khalid International Airport in Riyadh, killing one person and injuring 11, according to Saudi civil aviation authorities. The strike, which started a fire, occurred on a Sunday, following a deadly missile attack on the same facility on October 10, 2026, carried out by Yemen’s Iran-backed Houthi rebel group. That October 10 attack killed 13 people and injured more than 300, marking the deadliest attack on a Gulf country since the war with Iran began in March. The victims of the October 10 strike included four Saudi citizens, two Bangladeshi nationals, a Palestinian, a US citizen, an Egyptian, a Jordanian, a Syrian, and a Sudanese person. Following the recent projectile strike, air defenses intercepted a cruise missile in the vicinity of the airport. The Houthis claimed to have launched several attacks on the airport in the past week. In response to the attacks, Saudi authorities extended the closure of the airport, which is scheduled to remain closed until October 17. Additionally, authorities stated schools would be moved online until October 8, 2026. The British embassy in Saudi Arabia advised against all but essential travel to Jeddah and Dammam and recommended avoiding almost all airports. On Monday, a Saudi-led military coalition reported intercepting a ballistic missile headed for Riyadh, while a Houthi military spokesman claimed the group targeted the Riyadh airport with a ballistic missile and a military base in the south of the kingdom with a drone. The Houthis also claimed that Saudi Arabia struck the airport in Yemen’s rebel-held capital, Sana’a, on Sunday, though they provided no further details on damage or casualties."
  },
  {
    "text": "Following the October 10 strike on Riyadh airport, US President Donald Trump stated he was weighing whether to join the Saudi military campaign in Yemen, telling reporters, “We’re going to look into it.” The escalating attacks have raised concerns regarding the potential for more countries to be drawn into the Yemen conflict and the wider regional fallout of the US-Iran war, which has already spread to Lebanon and Iraq. The Houthi group is described as being allied with Iran and is engaged in a war with Saudi Arabia and the Yemeni government that Riyadh sponsors."
  }
]}

{
  "headline": "Russia pledges additional humanitarian aid to Lebanon and calls for immediate ceasefire",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Russian Foreign Minister Sergey Lavrov stated on Monday that Moscow will send additional humanitarian aid to Lebanon and called for an immediate ceasefire amid ongoing Israeli military operations. Speaking in Moscow following talks with Lebanese Foreign Minister Youssef Raji, Lavrov reaffirmed Russia’s support for the sovereignty, unity, and territorial integrity of Lebanon, according to reporting by Arab News citing the Russian state news agency TASS. Lavrov noted that almost 1 million Lebanese citizens have been seriously affected and described the humanitarian situation in the country as remaining tense. Russia previously sent humanitarian aid to Lebanon in April and is planning further deliveries."
    },
    {
      "text": "Lavrov welcomed the commencement of direct, US-mediated talks between Israel and Lebanon but asserted that the conflict must transition toward a political and diplomatic settlement. He called for the full implementation of UN Security Council Resolution 1701, which facilitated the end of the 2006 war between Israel and Hezbollah. The resolution mandates a cessation of hostilities, the withdrawal of Israeli forces from southern Lebanon as the Lebanese army deploys, and maintains an area between the Israeli border and the Litani River free of unauthorized weapons. Lavrov stated that the Israeli military was violating international law and fueling escalation. According to Lebanon’s Health Ministry, at least 4,386 people have been killed and 12,424 wounded since March 2."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Bangladesh government transfers unused Sonali Bag machinery to MBSTU",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The government of Bangladesh has transferred unused machinery from the Sonali Bag project to Mawlana Bhashani Science and Technology University (MBSTU) to support research into jute-based technologies and prevent the deterioration of public assets. State Minister for Textiles and Jute Shariful Alam handed the machinery over to the university’s vice-chancellor following the signing of a memorandum of understanding on October 8 between the Bangladesh Jute Mills Corporation (BJMC), the Bangladesh Climate Change Trust, and the university. The Sonali Bag project, which aimed to create a biodegradable alternative to plastic using jute cellulose, was scrapped on April 29, 2025, following a recommendation from a technical committee, according to a BJMC statement sent to The Daily Star. The Tk 100 crore project was deemed commercially unviable because production costs could not be reduced to acceptable levels; the statement noted that each bag would need to sell for Tk 25-40 to cover costs. Additionally, an 11-member committee found that producing five tonnes of Sonali bags required 40 tonnes of chemicals, which raised environmental concerns. Shariful Alam stated that the machinery will be used to support research into jute cellulose and eco-friendly products, noting that the facilities will be accessible to private researchers and may be used by the ministry for research and training. The minister added that the government is working with the environment ministry and other institutions to develop alternatives to polythene bags, suggesting that reusable jute bags could offer long-term savings despite higher initial costs."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Bangladesh Election Commission announces schedule for reserved women’s seat by-election",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The Election Commission (EC) of Bangladesh has announced a schedule for a by-election to a vacant reserved women’s seat in the 13th Parliament. An official notification signed by EC Senior Secretary Akhtar Ahmed was issued on Monday, setting November 12 as the voting day. The seat became vacant following the resignation of Ferdousy Ahmed Mishti, a lawmaker previously elected with the nomination of the BNP. Mishti resigned from her seat on October 1 following instructions from Prime Minister Tarique Rahman amid allegations of involvement in an attack, vandalism, looting, and demolition at the Radda MCH-FP Centre, a maternal and child healthcare facility in Mirpur, Dhaka, on September 24. Under current law, only the BNP can nominate a candidate for this vacant seat. If more than one candidate contests the election, only lawmakers from the BNP alliance will be eligible to vote, though reserved women’s seats generally feature a single candidate that requires no voting. The election schedule dictates that the deadline for submitting nomination papers to the returning officer at the Nirbachan Bhaban is October 26. Nomination papers are scheduled for scrutiny on October 27, with the final date for the withdrawal of candidature set for November 1."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "University of Ghana and KNUST postpone reopening due to administrator strike",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The University of Ghana (UG) and the Kwame Nkrumah University of Science and Technology (KNUST) in Ghana have postponed their reopening dates indefinitely. The decision follows a strike by the Ghana Association of University Administrators (GAUA), which has created uncertainty regarding the return of university administrators to their offices. According to a statement issued on October 10, 2026, signed by Professor Gordon A. Awandare, the Pro Vice-Chancellor, Academic and Student Affairs, the management of UG announced the postponement of the university's reopening, which had been scheduled for October 12, 2026. KNUST had scheduled its reopening for the day following the statement's issuance. The GAUA strike involves senior administrative staff, including registrars, admission officers, finance officers, and student affairs officers. The association has laid down its tools over unresolved concerns regarding allowances, conditions of service, and what it describes as unfair treatment compared to other staff categories within the universities. The strike has halted critical operations, including admissions, academic records management, registration, accommodation allocation, payroll management, accounting, human resources, corporate affairs, and related clerical work."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Syrian Post resumes domestic parcel routes and expands international services",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Syria’s state-operated postal service, Syrian Post, has resumed parcel deliveries between provinces and reopened international shipping routes, according to Arab News. Emad Al-Din Hamad, the director general of Syrian Post, stated that the organization resumed deliveries of parcels and goods between provinces in May after a gap of more than 10 years. The service currently accepts shipments weighing up to 30 kilograms through central post offices, with plans to gradually extend the network of offices. Since March, the organization has also expanded a fleet of mobile offices designed to serve remote towns, sparsely populated areas, and locations where services were temporarily interrupted by technical problems. These mobile offices provide services including pension payments, postal money transfers, and transactions using the Sham Cash digital-payment and mobile transfer application."
    },
    {
      "text": "Regarding international operations, Hamad said the international postal exchange office at Damascus International Airport has been repaired and returned to use in cooperation with Syrian Airlines, customs authorities, and the Universal Postal Union. International parcel services resumed on July 1, and the first incoming parcel from Jordan arrived on July 5. On October 1, the organization launched a service for sending parcels to worldwide destinations through the Universal Postal Union’s network. To expand cross-border e-commerce, Syrian Post reported it is working with Qatar Post and Qatar Airways to develop international mail transportation. The organization is also exploring Qatar Post’s “Connected” platform to facilitate purchases from online marketplaces in the US and Europe, and has held talks with Turkish Post regarding the connection of local delivery services with Turkish e-commerce platforms. Over the past year, Syrian Post has opened or reactivated 164 postal offices, consisting of 111 reactivated locations and 53 new locations. Hamad added that the organization is preparing to launch international money transfers."
    }
  ]
}
